ting immediately as somehow  a bad action that that should be avoided  and therefore uh sort of never do it  now in order to actually understand what  would happen if we introduce these  mechanisms we use this harvest domain  that  came from julian perola and colleagues  where you have some apples these are the  the green crosses in the picture they're  growing inside of a grid world  environment you have some agents in this  case there are there are 10 agents that  are the the red triangles that can go  around and each of the agents gets a  plus one reward whenever it eats an  apple  but the apples only renew if there's a  minimum density of other apples in their  vicinity and so if you eat a whole patch  of apples there will be no more apples  that uh that grow there  and these agents uh have in the original  implementation of the environment a  tagging beam the tagging beam  essentially  is like a weapon that incapacitates  agents in their neighborhood and  prevents them from from eating the  apples  but we added to this a gifting beam and  the gifting beam just takes a plus one  reward and spreads it around  uh to the agents that are within range  and so if you have uh you know only one  agent that's close to you you it will  get the entire reward but if you have a  couple of agents that are close to you  then this reward is going to be split up  among them  and uh this is all sort of uh the  parallel in terms of the training of the  agents the agents have a first-person  view of the environment  um  and so now we ran various experiments in  order to see which of these strategies  uh sort of prevent a tragedy of the  comments and what kinds of behaviors uh  come out  and so the first curves that i'm going  to show you are basically performance  curves these are the return uh per agent  in the in the left graph  and the sustainability and the right  graph sustainability in this case means  what is the average time between  positive environment rewards that the  a