nt  which is a controlled markov process so  this means it's just like an mdp but  doesn't have the reward function  specified yet but there are states there  are actions there are markovian  transitions  uh between the states conditioned on  action and it's all fully observable  the task is going to be one of the three  types of tasks that we discussed  and we're going to say that a reward  function  realizes the task in the environment  if the reward function leads to a value  function an associated long-term value  function  that respects a set of inequalities and  so  for the set of acceptable policies  what we want is the value  associated with good policies to be  superior to the value of bad policies  okay and here we have an initial state  as zero so we're comparing the values  with respect to that initial state  but obviously this can be also done with  with initial state distributions  in policy ordering we want the value  functions to be ordered according to  this policy ordering that's been  specified and in the case of trajectory  orderings we would like the returns  along these trajectories to be ordered  in in the order pre-specified  so in this paper we explored two  questions okay the first question is  when can a task be realized in a  controlled markov process  um and so in other words we wanted to  know given any task and any controlled  markov process environment is there a  mark of reward that realizes the task  and actually the answer to that is  negative  okay and i'm going to show you  one example one case in which in which  this is not really true  okay which is if you consider this  little grid world  um  if you say something like uh you know i  prefer policies that always go in the  same direction  okay  this is actually not expressible through  a markovian reward  policy itself  is markovian  okay because you can sort of specify the  action  but the we can't really express this  because actually there is some  non-markovian information t